
  # Method overloading vs overriding — compile-time vs runtime resolution.

  method overloading and method overriding are two types of polymorphism.

  The main difference is how Java decides which method to execute:
  - Overloading → Compile-time polymorphism: The compiler decides which method to call based on the method arguments.
  - Overriding → Runtime polymorphism: The JVM decides which implementation to execute based on the actual object type.
  Let's understand both with Java examples.
  1. Method Overloading (Compile-time polymorphism)
  Definition: When multiple methods have the same name but different parameter lists within a class, it is called
  method overloading.

  Parameters can differ by:
  - Number of parameters.
  - Type of parameters.
  - Order of parameters.

  2. Method Overriding (Runtime polymorphism)
  Definition: When a child class provides its own implementation of a method already defined in its parent class,
  it is called method overriding.

  Conditions:
  - Same method name.
  - Same parameter list.
  - Compatible return type (covariant return types are allowed).
  - Inheritance is required.
  - Access level cannot be more restrictive than the parent method.


